                <pb facs="1241" n="955" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg955"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/2739</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic"><date
                                ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                                calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1916-07-22T21:10:00-05:00"
                                notBefore="1916-07-17T18:00:00-05:00">undated</date></hi>.<lb/>[<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received July 22, 1916, 9.10 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4598. Your 3527, July 17, 6 p. m. I have again gone over <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >in extenso</hi> with Lord Robert Cecil, Minister of Blockade, his
                    Government’s refusal to admit American Red Cross supplies to Germany. He gave me
                    no definite hope of a reversal of his Government’s position, but he thoroughly
                    understands that much just criticism may be expected in the United States and I
                    think he sees that the British Government has made a needless and provoking
                    mistake, as I had previously informed them. My inference is that the Government
                    is afraid of public opinion, one strong section of which loudly demands the
                    exclusion of everything from Germany.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
